---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../data/site";
---
<BaseLayout title="How to Source Activewear Suppliers" description="Alibaba vs trade shows vs direct factory: a practical guide to finding and vetting reliable activewear suppliers. Avoid scams, verify quality, and build long-term relationships.">
  <section style="background:var(--color-primary);color:white;">
    <div style="max-width:56rem;margin:0 auto;padding:4rem 1.5rem;text-align:center;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:0.75rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
        <span style="font-size:1.5rem;">🔍</span>
        <span style="font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--color-accent-light);background:rgba(255,255,255,0.1);padding:0.2rem 0.75rem;border-radius:9999px;">Intermediate</span>
        <span style="font-size:0.75rem;color:#94a3b8;">11 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 style="font-size:2.25rem;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:1rem;">How to Source Activewear Suppliers</h1>
      <p style="color:#94a3b8;max-width:42rem;margin:0 auto;">Alibaba vs trade shows vs direct factory: a practical guide to finding and vetting reliable activewear suppliers. Avoid scams, verify quality, and build long-term relationships.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div style="max-width:56rem;margin:0 auto;padding:2.5rem 1.5rem;">
    <div style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:1rem;padding:2rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">The 4 Main Sourcing Channels</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;">Finding a reliable supplier is the most critical decision in your activewear business. A bad supplier can cost you thousands in defective products and lost customers. The four main channels each have pros and cons: (1) Alibaba/Made-in-China - broadest selection, easy to compare, but has many middlemen and scammers. (2) Trade shows - Canton Fair, MAGIC Las Vegas, Source Direct. Direct access to manufacturers. (3) Google search for factory websites - some factories have direct sales teams without Alibaba markup. (4) Referrals from other brands - the best source for verified, reliable suppliers.</p>
      
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How to Vet Suppliers on Alibaba</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;">undefined</p>
      <ul style="margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;"><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Trade assurance: Always use Alibaba Trade Assurance - your payment is held until you confirm receipt</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Factory vs trading company: Ask for a factory tour video. Trading companies mark up 15-30%</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Response rate: Above 90% is good. Below 70% means they are not active</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Years on Alibaba: 5+ years with consistent orders = more reliable</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Certifications: OEKO-TEX, BSCI, ISO 9001. Ask for copies, not just photos</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">MOQ requirements: Compare across 5+ suppliers to find the market rate</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Reviews: Look for reviews with photos - text-only reviews are often fake</li></ul>
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Questions to Ask Before Placing an Order</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;">undefined</p>
      <ul style="margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.5rem;font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;"><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Can I order samples first? (Minimum 1-5 pieces at what price?)</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What is your production lead time for 100/500/1000 pieces?</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Can you provide OEKO-TEX and BSCI test reports?</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Do you have a minimum order quantity for custom logo?</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">What payment methods do you accept? (Never pay 100% upfront)</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Can I visit your factory or do a video call?</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Do you have a quality control process? (3-step inspection?)</li><li style="margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Who are your current major clients? (Ask for references)</li></ul>
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Red Flags That Signal Scammers</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;">Protect yourself from the common scams: (1) Asking for 100% payment upfront with no escrow protection. (2) Prices too good to be true (50% below market rate). (3) Refuses to send samples or video call the factory. (4) No verifiable business license or export license. (5) Communicates off-platform after initial contact (they want to avoid Alibaba buyer protection). (6) Factory photos that look like stock images - ask for a live video tour.</p>
      
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-top:2rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Building a Long-Term Supplier Relationship</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.8;">Your goal is to move from transactional buyer to preferred customer. Steps: (1) Start with a small order (30-50 pieces) and test quality thoroughly. (2) Provide detailed feedback on what you liked and what needs improvement. (3) Commit to a monthly order volume (even small amounts) to build trust. (4) Pay on time - factories prioritize buyers with reliable payment history. (5) Share market feedback - tell them which styles are selling well so they can prepare inventory. Long-term partners get: better pricing, priority production, custom development, and flexible payment terms.</p>
      
      
    </div>

    <h2 style="font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-bottom:1.25rem;text-align:center;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0.75rem;margin-bottom:2.5rem;">
      
      <details style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:0.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
        <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;gap:1rem;">
          <span style="font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);font-size:0.9rem;">Is it better to use a trading company or a direct factory?</span>
          <svg style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;color:var(--color-text-light);flex-shrink:0;" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div style="padding:0.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.7;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);">Direct factories are generally better because: (1) Lower prices - no middleman markup, (2) More control over quality - you work directly with production, (3) Faster communication - no relay through a sales agent. However, some trading companies have better quality control and English communication. For your first order, start with a factory - if a trading company is significantly cheaper, it usually means they are adding markup on top of a factory deal anyway.</div>
      </details>
      
      <details style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:0.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
        <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;gap:1rem;">
          <span style="font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);font-size:0.9rem;">What is the safest payment method when sourcing from China?</span>
          <svg style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;color:var(--color-text-light);flex-shrink:0;" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div style="padding:0.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.7;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);">Use Alibaba Trade Assurance for your first orders - it protects your payment if the supplier fails to deliver. For larger orders ($5,000+), T/T 30% deposit + 70% before shipment with inspection is standard. Never pay 100% upfront to a new supplier. For established relationships, some factories accept Net-30 payment terms.</div>
      </details>
      
      <details style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:0.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
        <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;gap:1rem;">
          <span style="font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);font-size:0.9rem;">How do trade shows compare to online sourcing?</span>
          <svg style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;color:var(--color-text-light);flex-shrink:0;" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div style="padding:0.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.7;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);">Trade shows (Canton Fair, MAGIC, Source Direct) offer: direct access to factory owners (not just sales agents), ability to inspect product quality in person, faster relationship building (1 meeting = 10 email threads), and sometimes show-only discounts. Downsides: travel cost, limited time to vet, and you still need to verify after. We recommend using trade shows to find potential suppliers, then verifying and ordering samples through Alibaba to compare.</div>
      </details>
      
      <details style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:0.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
        <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;gap:1rem;">
          <span style="font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);font-size:0.9rem;">How do I verify a factory is real?</span>
          <svg style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;color:var(--color-text-light);flex-shrink:0;" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div style="padding:0.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.7;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);">Ask for: (1) A video call or live factory tour (any legitimate factory will do this), (2) Business license number and verify it on Chinese government business registration site, (3) Export license - factories that export have customs registration, (4) Sample order from a different address than your billing address to test their actual shipping process, (5) References from current international clients.</div>
      </details>
      
      <details style="background:white;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:0.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
        <summary style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:1rem 1.25rem;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;gap:1rem;">
          <span style="font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);font-size:0.9rem;">How many suppliers should I work with?</span>
          <svg style="width:1.25rem;height:1.25rem;color:var(--color-text-light);flex-shrink:0;" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <div style="padding:0.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);line-height:1.7;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);">Start with 1 primary supplier (your best performer) and 1-2 backup suppliers for redundancy. Too many suppliers spreads your volume too thin (losing volume discounts) and complicates quality control. As your business grows to 500+ pieces/month, you can add a third supplier to reduce risk. Never put all your eggs in one basket - if your supplier has a production issue, you need a backup to fulfill customer orders.</div>
      </details>
      
    </div>

    <div style="background:var(--color-secondary);border-radius:1.5rem;padding:2rem;text-align:center;">
      <h2 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--color-primary);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Have More Questions?</h2>
      <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-text-light);margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Get personalized answers for your specific business on WhatsApp.</p>
      <a href={SITE.whatsappLink} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem;background:var(--color-whatsapp);color:white;padding:0.875rem 2rem;border-radius:9999px;font-weight:700;">Ask on WhatsApp</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is it better to use a trading company or a direct factory?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Direct factories are generally better because: (1) Lower prices - no middleman markup, (2) More control over quality - you work directly with production, (3) Faster communication - no relay through a sales agent. However, some trading companies have better quality control and English communication. For your first order, start with a factory - if a trading company is significantly cheaper, it usually means they are adding markup on top of a factory deal anyway."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the safest payment method when sourcing from China?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use Alibaba Trade Assurance for your first orders - it protects your payment if the supplier fails to deliver. For larger orders ($5,000+), T/T 30% deposit + 70% before shipment with inspection is standard. Never pay 100% upfront to a new supplier. For established relationships, some factories accept Net-30 payment terms."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do trade shows compare to online sourcing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Trade shows (Canton Fair, MAGIC, Source Direct) offer: direct access to factory owners (not just sales agents), ability to inspect product quality in person, faster relationship building (1 meeting = 10 email threads), and sometimes show-only discounts. Downsides: travel cost, limited time to vet, and you still need to verify after. We recommend using trade shows to find potential suppliers, then verifying and ordering samples through Alibaba to compare."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I verify a factory is real?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Ask for: (1) A video call or live factory tour (any legitimate factory will do this), (2) Business license number and verify it on Chinese government business registration site, (3) Export license - factories that export have customs registration, (4) Sample order from a different address than your billing address to test their actual shipping process, (5) References from current international clients."
        }
    },
    {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How many suppliers should I work with?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Start with 1 primary supplier (your best performer) and 1-2 backup suppliers for redundancy. Too many suppliers spreads your volume too thin (losing volume discounts) and complicates quality control. As your business grows to 500+ pieces/month, you can add a third supplier to reduce risk. Never put all your eggs in one basket - if your supplier has a production issue, you need a backup to fulfill customer orders."
        }
    }
],
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": SITE.url },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Resources", "item": SITE.url + "/resources" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How to Source Activewear Suppliers", "item": SITE.url + "/resources/how-to-source-activewear-suppliers" },
    ],
  })} />
</BaseLayout>
